# Shell scripts run on the Linux/macOS CI runners (release.yml) — a CRLF shebang breaks them
# ("bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M"). Force LF regardless of a contributor's core.autocrlf setting.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Windows-only launchers stay CRLF.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# Engine patches must be byte-identical on every platform, for two independent reasons.
#
# 1. Their CONTENT HASH is load-bearing. tools/engine-patches/engine.lock.json (restructure item 29)
#    pins the patch-set hash so an export cannot silently use a template built from different patches.
#    Without a rule here the file is stored LF but checked out CRLF under core.autocrlf, so the same
#    commit hashes differently on Windows and on the Linux CI runner — and the lockfile check fails on
#    whichever platform did not write it, for a reason that looks nothing like line endings.
# 2. `git apply` is strict about them. A CRLF patch against LF sources fails to apply, or applies and
#    leaves CRLF in C++ files that had LF.
#
# eol=lf rather than -text: these ARE text (diffable, mergeable), they just must not be converted.
*.patch text eol=lf
*.diff  text eol=lf

# The lockfiles themselves, for the same "hash is load-bearing" reason plus a second one.
# release.yml keys the ~750 MB map cache on `hashFiles('data/maps.lock.json')`, so the file's BYTES
# decide whether three CI jobs hit or miss that cache.
#
# The failure this prevents is not hypothetical: writing this file from Python on Windows without
# newline="\n" silently converted 233 LF to CRLF, which turned a 12-line metadata edit into a
# 474-line whole-file diff and would have changed the cache key while changing no actual content.
# A reviewer sees "241 insertions, 233 deletions" on a lockfile and cannot tell that from a real
# re-pin of every pack.
*.lock.json text eol=lf
